// gujarati-demo — Gujarati script typesetting in texish.
//
// Render with:  texish-cli scripts/gujarati-demo.script
//
// Gujarati is written left to right, so it needs none of the bidirectional
// reordering Hebrew and Arabic do. What it needs instead is work inside each
// syllable. A consonant carries an inherent vowel; a following vowel sign (a
// matra) attaches above, below, before or after it; and a consonant joined to
// the next by the virama (U+0ACD) gives up its own vowel, taking a half-form
// or fusing with the next into a conjunct ligature.
//
// Of the scripts texish sets, Gujarati is the closest relative of Devanagari:
// the same consonant-and-virama model, the same half-forms and conjuncts, and
// the same reph — a syllable-initial ra drawn as a mark above the syllable —
// written without the shirorekha, the headline that joins Devanagari's letters.
// So the engine does the same work here: it segments the run into orthographic
// syllables, asks the bundled Noto Serif Gujarati font's GSUB table to build the
// half-forms and conjuncts, reorders the i-sign — typed after its consonant but
// drawn before the whole syllable — and lifts a syllable-initial ra out as the
// reph, which GPOS then places above the base along with the other marks.
//
// \font gujarati selects the bundled face, in a regular and a bold cut. No \rtl
// is needed — the script is left to right.

\use{document}

\title{The Gujarati Script in \TeXish}
\author{The texish engine}
\maketitle

\section{Setting a Gujarati paragraph}

Each syllable is shaped on its own and the syllables are set left to right. Within a
syllable the consonants may fuse into conjuncts and the vowel signs attach around the
base; between syllables the line breaker finds its breaks at the interword spaces exactly
as for Latin text.

\medskip

{\font gujarati 14 regular
ગુજરાતી એક સમૃદ્ધ ભાષા છે. તે ડાબેથી જમણે લખાય છે. દરેક અક્ષર એક વ્યંજન અને તેની
સાથેના સ્વરથી બને છે, અને બે વ્યંજન વિરામથી જોડાઈને સંયુક્ત રૂપ બનાવે છે.
}

\section{The pre-base i-sign}

The i-sign is typed after its consonant but drawn before it. In each word below the sign
appears at the left of its syllable even though it follows the consonant in memory ---
day, father, and knowledge:

\medskip

{\font gujarati 16 regular
દિન   પિતા   વિદ્યા
}

\section{Conjuncts and half-forms}

A consonant joined to the next by the virama gives up its vowel. Some pairs draw the first
as a half-form beside the base; others fuse into a single ligature glyph, as in love, holy,
and the akhand ligature ksha:

\medskip

{\font gujarati 16 regular
પ્રેમ   શ્રી   ક્ષ
}

\section{The reph}

A ra that opens a syllable and is joined by the virama is not drawn in place: it rises as a
mark above the syllable's base, after any vowel sign the base carries. These are duty,
action, sun, and meaning:

\medskip

{\font gujarati 16 regular
ધર્મ   કર્મ   સૂર્ય   અર્થ
}

\section{Independent vowels and signs}

A vowel that opens a syllable is written with its own independent letter, not a matra. The
vowel signs then stack around their base:

\medskip

{\font gujarati 16 regular
અ આ ઇ ઈ ઉ ઊ એ ઐ ઓ ઔ
}

\medskip

{\font gujarati 16 regular
કા કિ કી કુ કૂ કે કૈ કો કૌ
}

\section{A closing line}

A greeting and the city of Ahmedabad, set in the bold cut:

\medskip

{\font gujarati 18 bold
નમસ્તે! આ અમદાવાદમાં ગુજરાતી લિપિમાં લખેલું છે.
}
